    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>It was the single largest battle of the war and the largest in the world since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> in 1813, with around 285,000 Prussian troops against 240,000 Austrians on the Bohemian front.</li><li>Almost all the fighting on the Prussian side was done by the First Army and one guards division of the Second, and the Prussian needle gun, a breechloader, outpaced the Austrian muzzleloaders in rate of fire.</li><li>The Prussian victory cleared a path to German unification without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> a free hand when war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> came in 1870.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> had refused to sign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-geneva-convention">First Geneva Convention</a>, so its medical personnel counted as combatants and withdrew with the army, leaving the wounded on the field. Over 900 lay without care until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> nurse Marie Simon arrived several days later.</p></aside>
    </section>

