    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">June 1866</span><span class="rf-text">The Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> advances to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> and defeats the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> army of 23,000</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1866</span><span class="rf-text">Benedek reluctantly moves his army out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olmütz</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1866</span><span class="rf-text">Moltke orders the First and Second Armies to Jitschin</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1866</span><span class="rf-text">Prince Friedrich Karl defeats the Austrian I Corps at Jitschin</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1866</span><span class="rf-text">The Prussian armies defeat Benedek's army between Sadová and Königgrätz</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1866</span><span class="rf-text">The last skirmish is fought at Blumenau as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-prague-1866">Peace of Prague</a> is being signed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1866</span><span class="rf-text">The French ambassador presents Bismarck with a demand to relinquish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1870</span><span class="rf-text">War with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, in whose background lay the French demand for revenge for Sadová</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-koniggratz" data-v="2a54b2709e" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The armies take position either side of the river</b> The Prussians took their position west of the Bystřice river. The Austrian infantry stood partly fortified, with artillery behind it and cavalry in the rear, and the Saxons on the Austrian left.</li><li><b>Austrian guns pin down the Prussian right</b> The Austrian artillery opened fire on the Prussian right under Herwarth von Bittenfeld. The Saxons fell back in good order and fired on the advancing Prussians from higher ground. Around 10 am the advance guard of seven battalions was pulled back to the river.</li><li><b>The 7th Division advances into the Swiepwald</b> The Prussian centre went into the Swiepwald, where two Austrian corps met it. The First Army was ordered across the river to support the 7th Division, and Austrian artillery cut down the Prussians as they came out of the smoke.</li><li><b>Pöckh's charge is raked by flanking fire</b> The Austrian centre moved to flank the 7th Division, and Pöckh's infantry charge forced it back to the outskirts of the forest. Flanking fire from the 8th Division then raked his battalion and killed its commander.</li><li><b>The Second Army reaches the field</b> Crown Prince Frederick William arrived with the main bulk of the Second Army and hit the Austrian right flank as it retired from the Swiepwald. The 1st Guard came with it.</li><li><b>The Austrian line breaks at Chlum and Probluz</b> The Second Army broke through the Austrian lines and took Chlum behind the centre, and the 1st Guard reached the Austrian artillery. On the other side the Army of the Elbe broke through the Austrian left and seized Probluz.</li><li><b>Austrian cavalry cover the retreat</b> Benedek ordered a general retreat and a series of cavalry countercharges to back up his artillery and cover it. The cavalry kept the bridges over the Elbe open, at a cost of 2,000 men and almost as many horses.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At the outset of the war in June 1866 the Prussian armies were gathered along the Prussian border: the Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> under Karl Herwarth von Bittenfeld at Torgau, the First Army under Prince Friedrich Karl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> between Senftenberg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorlitz">Görlitz</a>, and the Second Army under Crown Prince Frederick William in Silesia west of Neiße (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Nysa</a>). The Austrian army under Ludwig von Benedek was concentrated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olmütz</a> (Olomouc). The campaign began with Herwarth von Bittenfeld's advance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, where he easily defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> army of 23,000 and joined with the First Army.</p><p>Benedek moved his troops out of Olmütz only reluctantly, on 18 June, marching north in three parallel columns with the I Corps protecting the right flank. On 22 June Helmuth von Moltke, Prussia's Chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">General Staff</a>, ordered both armies under his command to Jitschin (Jičín) near the Austrian positions, a daring manoeuvre undertaken to limit the war's duration despite the risk of one army being overtaken on the way.</p><p>Benedek was indecisive and failed to use his superior numbers to eliminate the Prussian armies one at a time. The Austrians were pressed back everywhere except at Trautenau (Trutnov), where they bested the Prussians despite great losses of their own. By 29 June Prince Friedrich Karl had reached Jitschin and inflicted a severe defeat on the Austrian I Corps under General Clam-Gallas, and the Crown Prince had reached Königinhof (Dvůr Králové) despite stiff resistance.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Königgrätz&#8221; cites where it covers Background, Prelude:</p><ul><li>G. Rothenberg, The Army of Francis Joseph (Purdue University Press, 1976)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Prussian forces totalled around 285,000 troops, whilst <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> had 240,000 men on the Bohemian front. Benedek had accepted the post of commander-in-chief with reluctance because he was unfamiliar with both the troops and the local terrain. On 3 July the Austrian army of 215,000 faced the Prussian Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> (39,000) and First Army (85,000); the Second Army under the Crown Prince, almost 100,000 men, was still on the march.</p><p>The Prussian side had superior training, Moltke's tactical doctrine and the Dreyse needle gun, a breechloader with a far higher rate of fire than the Austrian muzzleloading small arms and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>. It could be fired while prone and while moving quickly, whereas the Austrians had to stand up after each shot to reload their Lorenz rifles. The Prussians also made full use of railway transport, an innovation in European warfare. The Austrians relied heavily on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet">bayonet</a> charge. Their artillery had the longer range, and their cavalry was thought to be the better.</p><p>The Austrian infantry was partially fortified on hilly, wooded ground, with cavalry in the rear and artillery covering the approaches. Benedek drew his army up against the Elbe between Sadová and Königgrätz, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> on the Austrian left. The Prussians took position west of the Bystřice river. Moltke had ordered the Second Army to join the others where the Austrians were assembled, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> lines to it were out, and two mounted officers rode the 32 kilometres to deliver the order at 4 am.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i.jpg" alt="Wilhelm I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaiser_Wilhelm_I_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wilhelm Kuntzemüller, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle began at dawn in subsiding rain and mist. Shortly before 8 am the Austrian artillery opened fire, pinning down the Prussian right flank under Herwarth von Bittenfeld, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> on the Austrian left fired on the Prussian right from higher ground. In the centre the Prussian 7th Division under General Fransecky led the advance into the Swiepwald, where it met two Austrian corps, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a> ordered the First Army across the river to support it. A fierce fight followed in the forest. Austrian artillery fired into the smoke of the Prussian advance and cut down the 8th and 4th Divisions as they emerged from it. Benedek refused to call for a cavalry charge, which later commentators have argued might have won the battle.</p><p>At 11 am the Austrian centre moved to flank the 7th Division. Colonel Carl von Pöckh drove it back to the edge of the forest with an infantry charge, but flanking fire from the 8th Division destroyed his battalion as a fighting force and killed him. Close fighting in the forest negated Austrian advantages, as the artillery could not be trained on it and the IV Corps was committed piecemeal.</p><p>At 2:30 pm the Crown Prince arrived with almost 100,000 men and hit the Austrian right flank. The 1st Prussian Guard under Hiller von Gaertringen reached the Austrian artillery, the Second Army broke through and took Chlum behind the centre and the Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> broke through the left. Benedek ordered a general retreat and cavalry countercharges to cover it. They kept the bridges over the Elbe open and prevented pursuit, but 2,000 men and almost as many horses were killed, wounded or captured. Benedek crossed the Elbe near 6 pm.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/first-geneva-convention"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-geneva-convention.jpg" alt="First Geneva Convention" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/first-geneva-convention">First Geneva Convention</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henry_Dunant-young.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Frédéric Boissonnas, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle ended with heavy casualties for both sides. The Prussians had nearly 9,000 men killed, wounded or missing. The Austrians and their allies had roughly 31,000 men killed, wounded or missing, 9,291 of them prisoners, and lost 187 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> and 51 colours. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> had earlier refused to sign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-geneva-convention">First Geneva Convention</a>, so its medical personnel were regarded as combatants and withdrew with the main body of the forces, leaving the wounded to die on the field. Over 900 soldiers lay wounded without medical care until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> nurse Marie Simon arrived several days later with a large stock of supplies and tended them for 17 weeks. This experience led her and Carola of Vasa to establish the Albert Association, a precursor to the German Red Cross.</p><p>Dismayed by his earlier losses, Benedek had urged Emperor Franz Josef to make peace as the only way to save the army from a catastrophe. That evening he informed the emperor that the catastrophe had occurred. The Prussians pursued the Austrian Nord-Armee and fought a series of minor clashes, the last at Blumenau (today's Lamač, district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Bratislava</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>) on 22 July, just as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-prague-1866">Peace of Prague</a> was being signed.</p><p>After the victory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> attempted to extract territorial concessions in the Palatinate and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>. Bismarck told the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> on 2 May 1871 that on 6 August 1866 the French ambassador had presented what he called, to put it succinctly, an ultimatum: to relinquish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> or expect an immediate declaration of war.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is known above all through the movements and losses recorded for the armies. The decision to stand at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> turned on a telegram: when Franz Josef refused Benedek's request for peace, an ambiguous last sentence of the imperial telegram was interpreted as ordering a final stand. Bismarck's speech to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> of 2 May 1871 records the French demand of August 1866.</p><p>Several points are argued over. Later commentators have argued that a cavalry charge ordered by Benedek might have won the battle. The Prussian artillery was ineffective, and almost all the fighting on the Prussian side was done by the First Army and one guards division of the Second. The battlefield holds 475 memorials, some at the places where individuals, mostly officers, were killed and others at the mass graves in which Austrian, Prussian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> soldiers often rest together, put up with funds from families, regiments, officers, the Order of St. John and the Central Committee for the Preservation of Monuments from the War of 1866.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war.jpg" alt="Austro-Prussian War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schlacht-bei-koeniggraetz-von-georg-bleibtreu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Georg Bleibtreu, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Königgrätz was the decisive battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a>. It was the single largest battle of the war and the largest in the world since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leipzig">Battle of Leipzig</a> in 1813. The Prussian 7th Infantry Division and 1st Guards Infantry Division attacked and destroyed 38 out of 49 infantry battalions of four Austrian corps at the Swiepwald and at Chlum, and the Austrian army was forced to retreat at 3 pm, before any Prussian reinforcements could engage its flanks.</p><p>The victory gave Prussian statesmen a great opportunity. It cleared a path toward German unification, in particular with the Little <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> solution, Germany without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and the subsequent foundation of the North German Confederation. It also ensured that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> would have a free hand when a war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> came to pass in 1870. The French public resented the Prussian victory and demanded Revanche pour Sadova, revenge for Sadová, which formed part of the background to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>.</p><p>The Königgrätzer Marsch was written to commemorate the battle, and the memorials on the field and in its surroundings number 475. The experience of the wounded left on the field led Marie Simon and Carola of Vasa to found the Albert Association, a precursor to the German Red Cross.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Königgrätz on 3 July 1866 the Prussian armies defeated the Austrian army of Ludwig von Benedek, the largest battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a>. It cleared the way for a German state without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> a free hand against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1870.</em></p>

